# ClinicalInfoTeam rules — `clinical_information`

This is a single OBJECT section (not an array). It captures the clinical context
of the order: history, the reason the study was performed, and any extra
clinical detail. Source it from sections labelled "Clinical Information",
"Clinical History", "Reason for Study/Referral", "Pre-operative Diagnosis", or
the equivalent in the order header.

## 1. `clinical_history` (array of objects)

One object per distinct clinical-history entry, each with
`pre_operative_diagnosis` (VERBATIM as printed). `pre_operative_diagnosis` is the
stated diagnostic impression / pre-operative diagnosis — a *diagnosis* phrase
("Suspicious breast mass, R/O carcinoma", "Rule out lymphoma"), NOT the patient
presentation narrative (that is `clinical_finding_details`, see §4). Do not split
one history into several entries: a presentation sentence and its accompanying
diagnosis are the SAME history — the narrative goes to `clinical_finding_details`
and only the diagnosis phrase becomes a `clinical_history` entry. Emit one object
per genuinely distinct pre-op diagnosis; a single diagnosis → a one-element
array; none → `[]`.

## 2. `number_of_clinical_histories`

DERIVED — the length of the `clinical_history` array.

## 3. `reason_for_study`

VERBATIM — the stated reason for the pathology examination / referral
("Rule out JAK2 mutation", "Evaluate for malignancy"). `null` if not stated.

## 4. `clinical_finding_details`

VERBATIM — the patient clinical presentation / history narrative and any
additional relevant clinical detail not captured above (age + sex, presenting
symptoms, exam/imaging findings, prior treatment, relevant comorbidity as
printed) — e.g. "57-year-old female with a palpable right breast mass and
abnormal mammogram." This is where the descriptive presentation belongs; the
distilled diagnosis phrase from the same block belongs to
`pre_operative_diagnosis` (§1). `null` if none.

## 5. Do not pull diagnosis FINDINGS here

Histologic/molecular *findings* belong to the findings/biomarker/significant
sections. This section is the **pre-test clinical context**, not the result.
A line like "Final diagnosis: invasive ductal carcinoma" is NOT
`clinical_information` — leave it to the SpecimenFindingsTeam.

## 6. `page_number` + `llm_confidence_score`

`page_number` is DERIVED (where the clinical-information block sits). Emit a
top-level `llm_confidence_score` in [0.0, 1.0] for the section.

---

## Per-field rationale (`provenance`) — ALWAYS emit, as an ARRAY

Emit a `provenance` **array** on `clinical_information` — ONE entry per field you
populated. Each entry is `{field_name, block_id, page, type, rationale}` where
`field_name` is the populated field, `type` is one of
`verbatim` | `inferred` | `derived` | `absent`, and `rationale` is ONE short clause
saying WHY you chose that value (required when `type` is `inferred`/`derived`). This
is the per-field "why" the SME reviewer reads — emit it for EVERY field you
populated, keep it brief, ground it in a cited block, and never restate PHI beyond
the value itself. Use an ARRAY (not a keyed object).

```json
"provenance": [
  { "field_name": "primary_diagnosis", "block_id": "b5", "page": 1, "type": "verbatim",
    "rationale": "clinical history line states the diagnosis verbatim" }
]
```

Now run the ReAct loop and produce the `clinical_information` object in the exact
shape the base prompt specified.
